---
// Spoke (post-launch content, OFFPAGE_PLAN "content velocity" item 1,
// built 2026-10-12): vinyl (PVC) fence installation -- material spoke under
// the fence-installation hub. $ figures from src/lib/costs.ts only (the SAME
// vinyl per-foot range the hub and cost guide use; HomeBlue 2022 as the
// local cross-check it already is -- lessons-learned #5). Lifespan from
// costs.ts `lifespan` (Angi, Jul 2026). Heat: NOAA/NCEI State Climate
// Extremes Committee report (117 F at St. George, 10 Jul 2021, record-tying);
// vinyl UV/heat behavior: Ask the Builder "Vinyl Fencing Properties" and
// the Wikipedia "Synthetic fence" summary of TiO2 UV stabilizers, read
// 2026-10-12. Local rules: St. George City Code 10-18-1 / 10-18-5 as already
// cited on the hub (re-verified there 2026-09-26). Umbrella Service.
// Article + FAQ. Never says the site installs anything (compliance.md 3/6).
import GuideLayout from '../components/layout/GuideLayout.astro';
import IconList from '../components/sections/IconList.astro';
import CostTable from '../components/sections/CostTable.astro';
import { COST_AS_OF, perFoot, localCheck2022, fenceStain, lifespan } from '../lib/costs';

const path = '/vinyl-fence-installation-st-george-ut/';
const vinyl = perFoot.find((r) => r.key === 'vinyl')!;
const wood = perFoot.find((r) => r.key === 'wood')!;
const UPDATED = '2026-10-12';
const UPDATED_LABEL = 'October 12, 2026';

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'How much does a vinyl fence cost in St. George?',
    answer: `As of ${COST_AS_OF}, vinyl privacy fencing runs about ${vinyl.range} per linear foot installed, based on national published ranges. The one St. George-specific source, a June 2022 HomeBlue estimate, put 4-foot vinyl at ${localCheck2022.vinyl} per foot, so expect 6-foot privacy panels to land toward the upper half of the range.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'Does vinyl fencing hold up in St. George heat?',
    answer: 'Quality vinyl does, with two caveats. PVC softens in extreme heat, and St. George tied the all-time Utah high of 117 degrees in July 2021, so the panels you choose should be UV-stabilized, light in color, and installed with room to expand. Thin, dark, or recycled-content vinyl is where problems show up.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does a vinyl fence last?',
    answer: `Angi estimates ${lifespan.vinyl.range} for a vinyl fence, compared with ${lifespan.wood.range} for wood. Angi also notes that long UV exposure can make vinyl more brittle over time, which is why the formulation and warranty matter more in the desert than in a mild climate.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'Do I need a permit for a vinyl fence in St. George?',
    answer: 'Only in certain cases. St. George City Code §10-18-5 requires a permit for any fence in a front setback; a side or rear-yard fence that stays within the height limits of §10-18-1 (6 feet 4 inches in most zones) generally does not. Your HOA may require its own approval regardless.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I put a solid vinyl privacy fence in my front yard?',
    answer: 'Usually not. Under §10-18-1, most St. George zones cap front-yard fences at 4 feet, and within 10 feet behind the sidewalk only fences that are at least 50 percent open are allowed. A vinyl picket or ranch-rail style can meet that rule; a solid privacy panel cannot.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does vinyl fence need maintenance?',
    answer: `Very little. Vinyl never needs staining or sealing, where HomeGuide says wood needs sealing every 1 to 2 years at about ${fenceStain.perLinearFt} per linear foot. Rinsing off dust and checking gate hardware once a year is the usual routine.`,
  },
];

const heatChecklist = [
  { icon: 'sun', title: 'UV stabilizer in the cap layer', text: 'Ask whether the vinyl is co-extruded with a titanium-dioxide (TiO2) protected outer layer. UV breaks down untreated PVC; TiO2 at the surface is what stops it.' },
  { icon: 'tag', title: 'Light colors only', text: 'White, tan, and light gray stay cooler. Dark vinyl absorbs heat and can sag in a Phoenix-style summer; St. George summers are in the same class.' },
  { icon: 'frame', title: 'Virgin or factory-regrind vinyl', text: 'Avoid panels made from recycled vinyl of unknown origin; quality control is hard to hold through recycling, and that is where fading and brittleness start.' },
  { icon: 'ruler', title: 'Room for thermal movement', text: 'Vinyl expands when hot. Rails should float in the post pockets with a gap at each end; panels pinned tight will bow in July.' },
  { icon: 'blocks', title: 'Reinforced posts and rails', text: 'On 6-foot privacy runs, ask about aluminum or steel inserts in bottom rails and gate posts. Solid panels catch wind, and the inserts keep them straight.' },
  { icon: 'doc', title: 'A warranty that names fading and brittleness', text: 'Long warranties are common; read what is excluded. Heat, UV, and wind are the three things to check before you sign.' },
];

const styles = [
  { icon: 'fence', title: 'Privacy (solid tongue-and-groove)', text: 'The common 6-foot backyard panel. Best for sound and sight lines; most exposed to wind load.' },
  { icon: 'frame', title: 'Semi-privacy (lattice or spaced top)', text: 'A solid lower section with an open top that lets wind through and softens the look.' },
  { icon: 'scroll', title: 'Picket', text: 'Open design that can satisfy the city\'s 50-percent-open rule near the sidewalk, where solid panels are not allowed.' },
  { icon: 'flat', title: 'Ranch rail (2- or 3-rail)', text: 'Open, low-cost, and common on larger Washington County lots and horse property; no privacy.' },
];

const compareSteps = [
  { icon: 'ruler', title: 'Same height, style, and footage on every quote', text: 'A 6-foot privacy price and a 5-foot semi-privacy price are not comparable. Get the linear footage and gate count in writing.' },
  { icon: 'blocks', title: 'Post depth, spacing, and concrete', text: 'Ask how deep posts are set and how far apart. Wider spacing with no inserts is a lower bid that costs more in the first windstorm.' },
  { icon: 'doc', title: 'Manufacturer and product line named', text: 'A quote that says "vinyl" without a brand or series tells you nothing about the UV cap or wall thickness.' },
  { icon: 'house', title: 'HOA and permit handling', text: 'Confirm who submits the HOA application and, if the fence touches a front setback, who pulls the city permit.' },
  { icon: 'shield', title: 'License and insurance', text: 'Verify Utah license status with DOPL and ask for proof of insurance before any deposit. See the license guide below.' },
];
---

<GuideLayout
  title="Vinyl Fence Guide, St. George UT"
  description="Vinyl fence installation in St. George, UT: cost per foot, how PVC handles desert heat and UV, styles, city height and permit rules, and comparing quotes."
  path={path}
  eyebrow="Material guide · Fences · St. George & Washington County"
  h1="Vinyl Fence Installation in St. George: Cost, Heat, and the Rules"
  answer={`As of ${COST_AS_OF}, a vinyl (PVC) privacy fence in St. George, UT costs about ${vinyl.range} per linear foot installed and is estimated to last ${lifespan.vinyl.range} (Angi). In a city that has hit 117 degrees, choose UV-stabilized, light-colored panels installed with room to expand.`}
  updated={UPDATED}
  updatedLabel={UPDATED_LABEL}
  article={{ headline: 'Vinyl Fence Installation in St. George: Cost, Heat, and the Rules', datePublished: UPDATED }}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { href: '/fence-installation-st-george-ut/', label: 'Fence installation hub', blurb: 'All materials, heights, and permit rules.' },
    { href: '/fence-and-block-wall-cost-st-george-ut/', label: 'Full cost guide', blurb: 'Every range and source.' },
    { href: '/cmu-block-wall-vs-wood-fence-st-george-ut/', label: 'Block wall vs. wood fence', blurb: 'If you are weighing a wall instead.' },
    { href: '/hoa-fence-requirements-st-george-ut/', label: 'HOA fence requirements', blurb: 'Color and style approvals.' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Vinyl is the most-requested privacy fence material in newer St. George neighborhoods for a simple reason: it never needs
    staining, and it looks the same in year ten as it did on day one, if the right product was installed the right way. The
    desert adds two conditions that a mild-climate buyer never thinks about, heat and ultraviolet light, and those two decide
    whether a vinyl fence here is a 25-year purchase or a 7-year disappointment. This guide covers what vinyl costs in St.
    George, what to specify so it survives the summer, which styles the city allows where, and how to compare quotes from
    independent fence companies.
  </p>

  <h2>What vinyl fencing costs in St. George</h2>
  <CostTable
    caption={`Vinyl fence cost, as of ${COST_AS_OF}`}
    unit="per linear foot, installed"
    rows={[
      { label: 'Vinyl (PVC) privacy, national range', range: vinyl.range, note: vinyl.note },
      { label: 'Vinyl, 4-foot, St. George (HomeBlue, June 2022)', range: localCheck2022.vinyl, note: 'Older local estimate; cross-check only. Expect 6-foot privacy to run higher.' },
      { label: 'Wood privacy, for comparison', range: wood.range, note: 'Lower up front; needs sealing every 1 to 2 years.' },
    ]}
  />
  <p>
    Three things move the price inside that range: height (6-foot privacy panels use roughly half again the material of a
    4-foot picket), style (solid privacy costs more than ranch rail), and the number of gates, which are priced separately and
    usually carry the reinforced posts. No St. George fence company publishes prices, so these are dated published ranges, not
    local quotes; the <a href="/fence-and-block-wall-cost-st-george-ut/">cost guide</a> lists every source and date.
  </p>

  <h2>Vinyl in 117-degree heat: what to specify</h2>
  <p>
    St. George tied Utah's all-time high of 117 degrees on July 10, 2021, a reading NOAA's State Climate Extremes Committee
    formally accepted. Ordinary summers run above 100 for weeks. Vinyl is a thermoplastic: it expands as it warms and, by
    Ask the Builder's description, "performs like taffy" once the material itself reaches about 170 degrees, which a dark
    panel in direct sun can approach because vinyl stores radiant heat and runs hotter than the air. Ultraviolet light is the
    second problem; it breaks down untreated PVC, which is why manufacturers co-extrude a titanium-dioxide-rich outer layer
    to absorb it. None of this makes vinyl a poor choice here. It makes the product specification the whole decision.
  </p>
  <IconList items={heatChecklist} cols={2} />

  <h2>Vinyl fence styles and where the city allows them</h2>
  <IconList items={styles} />
  <p>
    Height and openness are governed by St. George City Code
    <a href="https://stgeorge.municipal.codes/Code/10-18-1" rel="noopener">§10-18-1</a>. In most zones, front-yard fences are
    capped at 4 feet and side and rear-yard fences at 6 feet 4 inches, and within 10 feet behind the sidewalk only decorative
    fences that are at least 50 percent open are permitted. That rule is why a vinyl picket works along the street while a
    solid privacy panel does not. Under
    <a href="https://stgeorge.municipal.codes/Code/10-18-5" rel="noopener">§10-18-5</a>, any fence in a front setback needs
    a permit; the <a href="/fence-permit-st-george-ut/">permit guide</a> covers the application. Washington, Hurricane, Ivins,
    and Santa Clara each have their own code; see the <a href="/washington-county-fence-service-areas/">service area guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>HOA color and style rules</h2>
  <p>
    Most newer St. George subdivisions are HOA-governed, and vinyl is where HOA rules bite hardest: many limit fence color to
    tan or an approved earth tone rather than white, cap height below the city's maximum, and require architectural approval
    before installation. Get the HOA's written fence standard before you get quotes, because a white 6-foot privacy fence that
    the city allows may still have to come down if the HOA did not approve it. The
    <a href="/hoa-fence-requirements-st-george-ut/">HOA fence guide</a> explains how HOA rules sit on top of city code.
  </p>

  <h2>Vinyl vs. wood over the life of the fence</h2>
  <p>
    Wood costs less up front at {wood.range} per foot, but HomeGuide says it needs sealing every 1 to 2 years at roughly
    {fenceStain.perLinearFt} per linear foot ({fenceStain.typical} for a typical 100 to 150 foot fence), and Angi puts its life
    at {lifespan.wood.range}. Vinyl at {vinyl.range} per foot needs no finish and is estimated at {lifespan.vinyl.range}. In the
    desert the gap tilts further toward vinyl, because unfinished wood dries, grays, and splits faster in low humidity and
    intense sun. If you are also considering a masonry wall, the
    <a href="/cmu-block-wall-vs-wood-fence-st-george-ut/">block wall vs. wood comparison</a> adds the third option.
  </p>

  <h2>How to compare vinyl fence quotes</h2>
  <IconList items={compareSteps} />
  <p>
    Confirm the company's Utah license status with the
    <a href="https://secure.utah.gov/llv/search/index.html" rel="noopener">DOPL license lookup</a> and ask for proof of
    insurance; the <a href="/licensed-fence-contractor-compare-st-george-ut/">license guide</a> explains what the record
    shows and the small-project exemption. If you want help finding independent local companies to quote, the
    <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> describes how matching works.
  </p>

  <p class="sgfw-note">
    Sources: NerdWallet, <a href="https://www.nerdwallet.com/home-ownership/home-improvement/learn/cost-to-install-a-fence" rel="noopener">"Cost to Install a Fence"</a> (updated July 10, 2025);
    HomeBlue, <a href="https://www.homeblue.com/fence-installation/saint-george-ut-fence-cost.htm" rel="noopener">"Fence Cost in Saint George, Utah"</a> (June 28, 2022; cross-check only);
    Angi, <a href={lifespan.vinyl.url} rel="noopener">"How Long Does a Vinyl Fence Last?"</a> ({lifespan.vinyl.date}) and
    <a href={lifespan.wood.url} rel="noopener">"How Long Does a Wood Fence Last?"</a> ({lifespan.wood.date});
    HomeGuide, <a href="https://homeguide.com/costs/cost-to-stain-paint-fence" rel="noopener">"Cost to Stain or Paint a Fence"</a> (updated February 4, 2026);
    NOAA NCEI, <a href="https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/monitoring-content/extremes/scec/reports/20220708-Utah-Maximum-Temperature.pdf" rel="noopener">State Climate Extremes Committee report, Utah maximum temperature</a> (July 2022);
    Ask the Builder, <a href="https://www.askthebuilder.com/vinyl-fencing-properties" rel="noopener">"Vinyl Fencing Properties"</a> (read October 12, 2026);
    St. George City Code <a href="https://stgeorge.municipal.codes/Code/10-18-1" rel="noopener">§10-18-1</a> and
    <a href="https://stgeorge.municipal.codes/Code/10-18-5" rel="noopener">§10-18-5</a> (checked September 26, 2026).
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
